<article class="env-lemma" id="02TR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02TR">Lemma <span data-tag="02TR">42.31.1</span>.</a> Let $(S, \delta )$ be as in Situation <a href="/tag/02QL" data-tag="02QL">42.7.1</a>. Let $X$, $Y$ be locally of finite type over $S$. Let $p : X \to Y$ be a flat morphism of relative dimension $r$. Let $i : D \to X$ be a relative effective Cartier divisor (Divisors, Definition <a href="/tag/062T" data-tag="062T">31.19.2</a>). Let $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{O}_ X(D)$. For any $\alpha \in \mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits _{k + 1}(Y)$ we have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  i^*p^*\alpha = (p|_ D)^*\alpha  \]
</div>
  <p> in $\mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits _{k + r}(D)$ and </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  c_1(\mathcal{L}) \cap p^*\alpha = i_* ((p|_ D)^*\alpha )  \]
</div>
  <p> in $\mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits _{k + r}(X)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $W \subset Y$ be an integral closed subscheme of $\delta $-dimension $k + 1$. By Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/056Q" data-tag="056Q">31.19.1</a> we see that $D \cap p^{-1}W$ is an effective Cartier divisor on $p^{-1}W$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/02TB" data-tag="02TB">42.29.5</a> we get the first equality in 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  i^*[p^{-1}W]_{k + r + 1} = [D \cap p^{-1}W]_{k + r} = [(p|_ D)^{-1}(W)]_{k + r}.  \]
</div>
    <p>
       and the second because $D \cap p^{-1}(W) = (p|_ D)^{-1}(W)$ as schemes. Since by definition $p^*[W] = [p^{-1}W]_{k + r + 1}$ we see that $i^*p^*[W] = (p|_ D)^*[W]$ as cycles. If $\alpha = \sum m_ j[W_ j]$ is a general $k + 1$ cycle, then we get $i^*\alpha = \sum m_ j i^*p^*[W_ j] = \sum m_ j(p|_ D)^*[W_ j]$ as cycles. This proves then first equality. To deduce the second from the first apply Lemma <a href="/tag/02T9" data-tag="02T9">42.29.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>